joining us now is the former head of the  British Army general Lord done it thank  you very much for being on the show this  morning Sophie good morning I'm really  interested to know what you think the UK  government should do should we be taking  back Islamic state fighters and putting  them on trial well I think usually I  disagree with what Donald Trump says but  on this occasion I think he's right if  there are as I think it's correct to say  a large number of foreign fighters in  captivity in Syria who originate from  countries like the United Kingdom then  they are our citizens and we have a  responsibility to act responsibly  towards them and I think that means that  they've got to come back to this country  they've got to be held while they talk  to and if there is sufficient evidence  against any of them or many of them then  they've got to put through be put  through due process and imprisoned if  that is the right thing to do but I  think it's also important that we treat  them fairly and we treat them with  justice but tempered with a bit of mercy  as well because I think the the way we  treat them may well have important  significance for the way other people  view our society we don't want to see  others being radicalized and going off  overseas and in the future so I think  how we treat these people coming back  we've got to treat them fairly but  firmly I think it's important that we  get that right so in this case I think  Donald Trump is right there our  responsibility we've got to take them  back I mean this has really come into  the news recently because of the  discovery of Shamima Begum the girl who  left Bethnal Green to join Islamic state  in Syria lots of kind of members of the  public feel that she shouldn't be able  to come back but from what you're saying  it sounds like you think that actually  she's the British citizen so she should  be well you've got to turn it around and  say if she's not going to come back here  where is she going to go she can't stay  in a in a refugee camp in Syria for the  rest of her life it's not right to say  that well Americans can take her and the  other fighters and stick them in  Guantanamo if these people are British  French German citizens they're in  Britain France Germany or whatever ever  other country has to take responsibility  for their citizens  now taking responsibility it means that  we bring them back we talk to them we  investigate them and if there are  charges that can properly be laid  against them then that should be the  case and they should be put through the  judicial process and punished  accordingly  but I think it's important that we do  treat these people fairly as I say I  think how we treat them will be seen by  other minority populations in our  country and that will affect how they  behave in the future it's really  important we get this right the problem  is though it's proven very difficult to  actually put suspect as members of  Islamic state through trial and actually  put these people in prison hasn't it and  you can see why members the public might  be very worried about the threat to our  own national security if they return  home I completely agree with that I mean  I think it's a real challenge for our  authorities but it's but just because  it's a challenge doesn't mean to say we  shouldn't rise to it and try and get it  right  they've been radicalized over a period  of time they can be talked to there are  some who may will want to change their  way their lives are led and live more  conventionally there are others that  perhaps will never change their mind in  which case they are likely to be in  prison for quite some time each case has  got to be treated separately and judged  on its own merits but without repeating  myself unduly I think how we treat these  people is really important for the  effect it will have on others in the  future and on our own society and I  think the big challenge for us and we  were saying this back in 2014 2015 is to  ask ourselves why with all the  opportunities in this country what a lot  of young people want to forsake all that  and go away on fight and fight in  somewhere like Syria and Iraq I think  the challenge for our society is to make  it sufficiently attractive enough  opportunity for people through education  and jobs and living a better life  that they want to stay and make a future  in this country and not feel they've got  to go and chase some errant dream  elsewhere I'm interested to know your  thoughts on the current situation in  Syria as well because Donald Trump's  also tweeted to say we're pulling back  after a hundred percent Caliphate  victory has there been a hundred percent  victory and are you happy to see the  u.s. pulling back their troops in Syria  well I think there are several issues  there the Caliphate in 2014-15 made a  strategic error which has worked to our  advantage in that they chose to occupy  large sways of territory and in doing  that they then provided themselves set  themselves up if you like as a physical  target that could be attacked and over  the last four or five years that's  what's happened their territory has  gradually been reduced their towns and  cities have been recaptured Raqqa most  prominently fell quite some time ago and  they are reduced to a very small little  bit of territory which will probably be  overrun by Allied forces really quite  soon that doesn't end the Caliphate idea  it's an ideology it's a dream it's a  vision and it will reoccur elsewhere I'm  afraid that's just a fact of 21st  century life the fundamentalist Islamist  movement that we've been combating and  seen so sort of all over the place since  9/11 is going to pop up elsewhere we're  seeing it particularly in sub-saharan  Africa at the present moment and that is  a concern as we will probably see it  elsewhere in the Middle East so it's not  it's not a hundred percent victory we  may well complete the recapture of the  territory that they had but the ideology  the thinking the support behind it that  is going to continue and that will  become or will remain the struggle for  this generation perhaps even the next  generation as well and now it feels like  I can't get through an interview in a  minute without mentioning breakfast so  I'm interested to know your thoughts on  whether Britain's role in the world is  going to be increased or diminished  after we leave the European Union well  given we are going to leave the European  Union on the 29th of March in some shape  or form and we'll have a continuing  relationship with Europe again of some  shape or form almost by definition we  are therefore saying we want to pay a  more global role and we want to be  trading with Commonwealth countries with  if we can restore relations with China  after the aircraft carrier business we  all want to do that trade with India so  we will want to take a more global  position and I think that's a perfectly  reasonable  thing for us to do and it's not just  about trade I think it's about influence  as well and we have to work out how  we're going to exercise that influence  and I think there's an important  harmonization between our diplomatic  skills our defense capabilities and the  way that we use our development money as  well as trade I think put all those  things together and that is the way the  UK should use its influence been  officially in the world in the future as  well as improving our trading  opportunities now you've served in  Belfast in the early 1970s and in your  memoirs you wrote that this was not just  writing and civil disorder it was war so  from your wealth experience over across  the sea what would be the consequences  of a hard border between Ireland and  Northern Ireland and are you worried  that politicians are putting the peace  process at risk  well the peace process must not be put  at risk just to go back to the start of  your comment there in 1972 alone we lost  102 British soldiers killed in the  province of Northern Ireland that's a  figure that was only exceeded once of  the combined operations in Iraq and  Afghanistan in more recent times so a  tremendous amount of blood and treasure  was expended in making life in Northern  Ireland much more peaceful much better  for the inhabitants of of Northern  Ireland so whatever happens we must make  sure that we don't provide conditions  again whereby Northern Ireland can  regress to a position akin to the  troubles which went on for such such a  long time so this whole business of a  hard border there must not be a hard  border  I'm afraid I'm one of those that  believes that with imagination and  determination there are ways that can be  produced which means that we can can  continue to trade between Northern  Ireland and the Republic of Ireland  without having to go to men with  clipboards and flat caps standing at  country checkpoints frankly if you buy a  newspaper in its barcoding I can't  really see why goods and services can't  be identified traced taxed as necessary  at their points of origin and don't have  to be taxed at the point at which they  cross an international boundary  um well let's fingers crossed hope  you're right and the defense secretary  moving on made a pretty forward speech  this week shall we say he said he wants  to see increased lethality the chances  now to abandon a trip to China after he  said he wanted to send a warship into  the position Pacific Russia have dubbed  him the Minister for war rather than the  Minister for defence do you think he  needs to tone it down a bit  well Gabby Williamson is a very  enthusiastic individual and he's taken  his portfolio defense seriously yes I  think we all know he's pretty ambitious  as well and wants to use defence as a  platform to develop his own career I'm  afraid a number of politicians do that  he's also recognized I think quite  correctly that we do not spend enough on  defense in this country when Philip  Hammond was a sector State for Defence  seven eight nine years ago we were  spending about 2.4 percent of GDP on  defense and now it is just down to 2  percent so Gavin Williamson is trying to  make the case for us to spend more on  defense was he right to say we're going  to send our aircraft carrier off to the  Spratly Islands to be honest I don't  think years  I think actually it's premature the  aircraft carrier Queen Elizabeth is a  very fine looking ship but it hasn't got  its full complement of airplanes yet so  let's get the thing properly equipped  properly sorted out and then use it  responsibly just because we've got it  doesn't mean to say we use it most  defense capabilities we hold in  readiness for something that really  demands them to be used we don't just  use it or send it because we can so I  think come I think you may have actually  just over sold that one over the last  few days and if it has had the  disappointing effect the Chancellor's  trip to China largely going to be  talking about trade and that kind of  thing if that's if that's been cancelled  result then that's a bad bad diplomatic  move and then Gavin's got that one wrong  okay now while we've got you I have to  ask you and this week the Shadow  Chancellor John McDonnell said that  Churchill was a villain because of his  role in tony pandey so i'm going to put  the same question to you  winston churchill hero or villain  well I'm an unreconstructed fan of  Winston Churchill and as far as I'm  concerned he made virtually every  mistake in the book which he learned for  a lot of those experiences as he went  through his career but he was the right  man for this country in May 1940 and I  often think if Lord Halifax had become  Prime Minister I think he'd have made a  separate peace with Hitler in May 1940  and they may well have come after us the  following year I think the map of Europe  would look very different and the brexit  debate we're having would probably be  completely irrelevant okay Lord done it  thank you very much we're very beautiful  but surely looking Norfolk I'll let you  go and warm up thank you

